// cursor-boundary.maxtest — hover and `textDocument/definition` resolve ONE cursor position, and this
// is what says so.
//
// A caret at a token boundary touches two tokens, and both methods must name the same one: with the
// caret one past `name` in `let z = name#if os(Windows)`, that is `name`. An editor that jumps somewhere
// it will not describe is the defect.
//
// Both methods ask one resolver, `LspSymbols.lspTokenAtCursor`, and its rule is that an identifier ENDING
// at the offset beats a token BEGINNING there. Half-open containment counts `offset == start` as
// "inside", which is the caret at a token's LEFT edge rather than within it, so containment-first would
// describe the directive the author had just typed past. Strict containment and ending-at-an-identifier
// are mutually exclusive, so this boundary is the ONLY position the two rules decide differently.
//
// See `LspClient.maxon` for why every session is released before anything is asserted.

// A keyword begins where the name ends, so hover could describe the conditional-compilation directive
// where the author was pointing at the name.
function boundaryDirectiveFixture() returns String
	return String.from(b"let name = 1
let z = name#if os(Windows)
#endif")
end 'boundaryDirectiveFixture'

// One past the `e` of `name` on the second line — `let z = name` is twelve characters.
//          111111111122
// 0123456789012345678901
// let z = name#if os(Windows)
let BoundaryCursorLine = 1
let BoundaryCursorCharacter = 12

// The line `let name = 1` declares it.
let BoundaryDeclarationLine = 0

test 'hover and definition name the same token at a boundary'
	boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration(boundaryDirectiveFixture(), what: "a `#if` directive")
end 'hover and definition name the same token at a boundary'

function boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration(source String, what String) throws TestFailure
	try expectDefinitionAt(source, line: BoundaryCursorLine, character: BoundaryCursorCharacter, startLine: BoundaryDeclarationLine, note: "definition at the end of `name`, where {what} begins, must reach the declaration of `name`.")

	let rendering = try hoverRendering(source, line: BoundaryCursorLine, character: BoundaryCursorCharacter)
	let declaration = "let name = 1"

	try Expect.contains(rendering, needle: declaration, message: "hover at the end of `name`, where {what} begins, described something else. Definition jumps to `name` from this very position, so a hover describing anything else is an editor jumping somewhere it will not describe.")
end 'boundaryBothNameTheDeclaration'
